From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 18:30:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] replay: x-rr-begin-record and x-rr-begin-replay, recording a
 running VM

PANDA's begin_record on QEMU's own engine and log format.  x-rr-begin-record
stops a TCG VM, switches it to icount if it was started without
(icount_enable_runtime: the virtual clock continues, CF_USE_ICOUNT on every
vCPU, the icount TCG ops, a TB flush), adds filter-replay to every netdev
that has none, journals the chosen chardevs (default serial*/parallel*),
saves the start snapshot, reloads it (loading is not the identity: an
overdue timer becomes vCPU work) and opens the log.  It refuses virtio
transports with ioeventfd on (kicks would bypass the log, and it cannot be
turned off after realize) and reports writable drives not behind blkreplay.

x-rr-begin-replay does the same in a process started with -S and the same
argv, then replays from the snapshot: a log begun at run time does not
contain machine init, which rr=replay would replay.

A begin that fails undoes itself (no log; icount switched off again if it
switched it on), with icount_disable_runtime.

Loading the start snapshot needs run_on_cpu() to hand the replay mutex to a
waiting vCPU (x86 vapic_post_load), which 0028 does by dropping and
retaking the BQL.

From observe's rrux spike (replay/replay-begin.c), R3 of the panda-next
plan.
---
